alice_csm: ParcelLab onboarding — their AWS Lambda-based worker farm is getting 429s right after deploy. they call our SDK on coldstart and see ~200 concurrent preflight requests. customer flow blocked.
marco_support: account: parcellab-prod, hosted. any response body or headers?
alice_csm: pasted sample: `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\",\"reason\":\"concurrent_preflight_exceeded\"}`. no Retry-After header captured.
riley_devex: common Lambda anti-pattern: fetching ephemeral token inside handler on every invocation. put token in module/global scope (outside handler) so warm instances reuse it. also keep a tiny in-memory queue to cap concurrent preflight calls to 3.
devon_backend: agree. sending a tiny Node lambda pattern we recommend. also add x-install-id so we can correlate per-install fanout.
riley_devex: snippet: ```
// module scope
let cached = null
let expiry = 0
const inflight = []

async function getToken() {
  if (Date.now() < expiry && cached) return cached
  const res = await fetch('https://auth.parcellab.com/ephemeral')
  cached = await res.text()
  expiry = Date.now() + 45000
  return cached
}

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  // simple concurrency cap
  while (inflight.length >= 3) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 50))
  inflight.push(1)
  try {
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.random()*200)) // jitter
    const token = await getToken()
    return fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/sessions', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}`, 'x-install-id': event.installId } })
  } finally { inflight.pop() }
}
```
